ence is available, use it to start optimization. Otherwise, generate candidates of length 512.\n\n 2. Phase 1: Temperature Annealing (20% of budget):\n   - Set a list of increasing temperatures [5, 10, 20, 50] and for each temperature, run a projected L-BFGS-B optimization on the sequence coefficients.\n   - The objective for Phase 1 is approximated by:\n         obj = (sum_i exp(-a_i\u00b2/T))   [a metric per temperature]\n   - But then we compare to the exact objective.\n\n 3. Phase 2: Progressive Step Size with Temperature Hypergradient (40% of budget):\n   - Use a sequence mean constraint (project_simplex) and a block-wise stochastic coordinate descent for local minima.\n   - Break the candidate inversion into blocks with different lag constraints, then update the coefficients.\n\n 4. Phase 3: Local Minimization with Simplex (60% of budget):\n   - Update the sequence using projected gradients, where the constraint is computed via the max-convolve.\n   - Use a multi-stage constraint optimization:\n\n      a. Compute the full convolution and take the indices of the top peaks.\n      b. For each top peak, compute the set of contributing elements (screeners).\n      c. Using these screeners, project the sequence so as to lower the measured objective.\n\n 5. Surrogate Objective:\n\n      def approx_objective(a, T):\n          # Smooth the max in the convolution using a soft-max in log-space for a given temperature T.\n          # Then return an initial score.\n\n 6. Optimization for a candidate during Phase 3:\n\n      minimization_a L-BFGS-B for the exact objective, but since `max(convolve(a))` introduces a hinge constraint, we use:\n\n        def soft_max_constraint(a, T):\n            # This is the soft hinge constraint, which penalizes the large peaks in the convolution.\n\n      Then constrained minimization is performed.\n\n 7. Starting from a base candidate (parent or uniform), we:\n\n        - Increase the sequence length to multiples of the starting length (like doubling) to try to reduce the normalized peak-max.\n\n 8. Construction and Statistical Process:\n\n      - We generate between 20 and 200 candidates, depending on the length.\n      - Each candidate is of length n (starting from 512, then increasing to 1024, 2048, etc.)\n      - We then perform numerical optimizations at each length using the projected gradient descent.\n\n 9. Metric to Track:\n\n      - Primary: The minimal value found by the team's author previous program was 1.505056.\n\n 10. New Objective to Reach:\n\n      - Target: 1.5027\n\n 11. User Interaction and Timeout:\n\n      - We will enforce strict time checks: 800 seconds per optimization trial.\n      - Use adaptive reloading and multiple restarts if necessary.\n\n 12. Final Validation and Selection:\n\n      - After optimization, we re-evaluate the candidate with the exact `evaluate_sequence` function.\n      - Only keep candidates that have scores below the current global minimum.",
